"""Stevens & Lewis F-16 data (NASA TP-1538, Nguyen et al. 1979, as tabulated in Stevens & Lewis,
*Aircraft Control and Simulation*, Appendix A). The numbers are the table subroutines of the book's
Fortran listing as reproduced in the open-source AeroBenchVV Python port (Bak et al.,
`code/aerobench/lowlevel/*.py`), extracted verbatim. Raw layout, as in the book: every list row is
one value of the second argument, every column one of the 12 angles of attack -10, -5, ..., 45 deg.

  CX, CM          rows: elevator -24, -12, 0, 12, 24 deg
  CZ              CZ(alpha) only (the elevator and sideslip terms are formulas)
  CL, CN          rows: |beta| 0, 5, ..., 30 deg (odd in beta: the sign of beta is applied)
  DLDA ... DNDR   rows: beta -30, -20, ..., 30 deg (aileron and rudder effectiveness)
  DAMP            rows: CXq, CYr, CYp, CZq, Clr, Clp, Cmq, Cnr, Cnp
  THRUST_IDLE/MIL/MAX
                  rows: Mach 0, 0.2, ..., 1.0; columns: altitude 0, 10000, ..., 50000 ft (lbf)

Two corrections against the AeroBenchVV port: its CL rows at alpha = 15, 20, 25 deg and its CZ at
alpha = 5 and 20 deg (-0.415, -1.355) are not the book's; the textbook values are used here (they are
also the values of the archimedes F-16 tutorial). With them the reference model reproduces the book's
check of the F16 subroutine to 7 digits and its Table 3.6-3 trims (design.py). Two damping entries
still differ between the public ports and cannot be decided by any published check: CXq at
alpha = -5 deg (-0.110 here, +0.110 in archimedes) and CYr at alpha = 40 deg (-0.493 here, +0.493
there). Neither is used by the longitudinal scenarios of this example.
"""

